#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091944 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092338 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e380013-0002-5a17-bbf7-e9ce89e6331d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Indicator Function of a Set"}] :question/id #uuid "2acf7f4e-1d5d-56e5-8478-9611a3afde73" :question/math-academy-id "e-12766" :question/problem "![](images/45/45a423930fb07c365f10cdfbdeb3d45ef337078fa77e1b0699388088438d4c70.png)\n\nWhat is the subset $A$ of $U=\\{1,2,3,4,5 \\}$ whose indicator function $\\boldsymbol{1}_A(x)$ is shown above?" :question/worked-solution "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1$, depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X$. Then, the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n${1,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]x \\in A[/math], \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]x \\notin A[/math]$.\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $1,2$, and $5$ are mapped to $1$. Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,2,5 \\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091945 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092339 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd399539-33cf-5738-b970-fcc6c1d919c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using Properties of Indicator Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "cc45cc10-61e3-5cb7-babc-7253a5158c4b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12767" :question/problem "$\\mathbf{1}_{A}(-1) = 0, \\qquad \\mathbf{1}_{B}(-1) = 1$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 1$\n2. $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 0$\n3. $-1 \\in A \\cup B$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overline{A}$, $A \\cap B$, and $A \\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1_{A^(―)}(x) &= 1 - 1_{A}(x) \\\\\n1_{A∩B}(x) &= min{1_{A}(x), 1_{B}(x)} \\\\\n1_{A∪B}(x) &= max{1_{A}(x), 1_{B}(x)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1_{A∪B}(-1) &= max{1_{A}(-1), 1_{B}(-1)} \\\\\n&= max{0, 1} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 1$, we conclude that $-1$ belongs to $A \\cup B$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092338 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e380013-0002-5a17-bbf7-e9ce89e6331d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Indicator Function of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c2ad763-0093-58d0-b242-b45afa1b276c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a, b, d}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089772} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ac4e9f-ae1f-56c2-abad-036ee1d4b81f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{d, e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089773} {:answer/id #uuid "faf83bd6-e694-5461-92bf-96b2ebeaf9d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a, b, c, d}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089774} {:answer/id #uuid "08d43544-6d32-5f94-9cb2-977cc3bc4961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{c, e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089775} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec409c9-9da8-57e6-8151-dac8668f101e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a, b, c}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089776}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c6e70036-1f48-547b-aa8a-ed6be6496ff6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 175921